<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <!--
      Note for anyone editing the comments here: the rest of this repository
      punctuates an aside with a doubled ASCII hyphen, and XML forbids exactly
      that sequence inside a comment. The manifest merger then rejects the whole
      file with "Error parsing AndroidManifest.xml" and says nothing about which
      line or why. The em dashes below are therefore load-bearing rather than a
      style choice, and the encoding declaration above exists to make them
      explicit.
    -->
    <!-- The Flutter template only declares INTERNET in the debug and profile
         manifests (the tool needs it for hot reload), which is a classic trap: the
         app talks to the server on every screen, so a release APK built without
         this line installs fine and then fails every request with an opaque socket
         error. Declared here so it applies to all build types. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"/>

    <!--
      ===== Local reminders (F1) =====

      Everything below exists because of one product fact: a reminder that does
      not arrive makes this app pointless (see flutter-migration-plan.md, the
      section on why the PWA was abandoned). The previous PWA failed precisely
      here, so each of the lines below is a place where the feature silently
      stops working if it is absent.

      POST_NOTIFICATIONS is declared by the plugin itself since its v16, but it
      is repeated here on purpose: it is the permission the app has to *ask* for
      at runtime on Android 13+, and a reader of this manifest should see that
      the app posts notifications without having to go read a dependency's
      manifest. When it is denied, nothing throws — zonedSchedule succeeds, the
      alarm fires, and the system drops the notification. That silence is why the
      bench screen displays the flag.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS"/>

    <!--
      Without this, Android forgets every scheduled alarm the moment the phone
      reboots, and there is no error anywhere: the queue is simply empty
      afterwards. Together with the ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver below, the
      plugin re-arms everything it had stored. "Survives a reboot" is an explicit
      acceptance criterion of F1 — a phone that reboots overnight for a system
      update must still produce the 09:00 reminder.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED"/>

    <!--
      Exact alarms, Android 12+.

      A day-granularity reminder does not need minute precision in principle,
      but an *inexact* alarm is batched with other wakeups and can drift by
      hours. "Напомнить утром" arriving at 14:00 is a different, much less useful
      product, so we start from exact and degrade only if the permission is
      missing (LocalNotificationGateway picks inexactAllowWhileIdle then, and the
      bench says so out loud).

      Both permissions are declared, and that is not redundancy:

      - SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM is auto-granted on Android 12 but is user-revocable
        from Android 13 on, so the app must be able to ask for it
        (requestExactAlarmsPermission opens the system "Alarms & reminders" page);
      - USE_EXACT_ALARM is granted at install time and cannot be revoked, which
        is what makes the overnight test meaningful — but Google restricts it to
        apps whose core function is alarms/calendars and audits it on Play.
        TaskRadar is sideloaded onto one personal phone and will never be
        published (see flutter-migration-plan.md, F5), so the restriction does
        not apply. If that ever changes, drop this line and keep the one above.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.USE_EXACT_ALARM"/>

    <!--
      ===== Voice input (F9) =====

      Dictation into the sandbox. The recognition itself happens on the device —
      the model is an ONNX file in the app's own storage and no audio leaves the
      phone — but recording still needs the runtime permission, asked for by
      `record` the first time the microphone button is held.

      Nothing else is declared here on purpose. In particular there is no
      foreground-service or background-recording setup: the microphone is open
      only while a finger is on the button and while the app is in front, which
      is both the product decision (see `widgets/dictate_button.dart`) and the
      reason this permission cannot quietly become a listening device.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO"/>

    <!--
      ===== Task editor: place reminder (design/task-editor-spec.md §7) =====

      "Напомнить по месту" is an Android geofence (native_geofence over
      GeofencingClient), enter transitions only. FINE for a radius of 100 m to
      mean anything, COARSE because Android 12+ lets the user grant only that
      and the request must name both. BACKGROUND because a geofence registered
      without it never fires while the app is closed, which is the only time
      it matters; on Android 11+ it cannot be asked for in a dialog at all, so
      the settings screen sends the user to the system page for "Allow all the
      time". Nothing here is asked for until the user turns the feature on.
      Pictures need no permission: image_picker uses the system photo picker.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION"/>
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION"/>

    <!--
      The label and cleartext flag are placeholders only so that the emulator
      QA package (`build.gradle.kts`, `qaBuild`) can say "TaskRadar QA" and talk
      plain HTTP to its synthetic API on 10.0.2.2. For every other build they
      resolve to "TaskRadar" and "false", which is the platform default anyway.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="${appLabel}"
        android:usesCleartextTraffic="${cleartextTraffic}"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher">
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:taskAffinity=""
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <!--
              Task editor (§6): "Поделиться" a picture from another app into a
              task. Read by share_handler; singleTop above means a
              share while the app is open arrives through onNewIntent instead
              of starting a second copy.
            -->
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/*"/>
            </intent-filter>
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/*"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!--
          The two receivers from flutter_local_notifications. Since the plugin's
          v16 they are NOT declared by the plugin's own manifest, so an app that
          forgets them compiles, installs, schedules alarms that appear in
          `pendingNotificationRequests()` — and shows nothing when they fire.
          There is no warning of any kind.

          ScheduledNotificationReceiver is what actually posts a scheduled
          notification when its alarm goes off.

          ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver re-arms everything after the events
          that wipe the alarm queue. BOOT_COMPLETED is the obvious one;
          MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED matters just as much during development, where the
          app is reinstalled several times a day and would otherwise silently
          lose the very alarm being tested. The two QUICKBOOT actions are the
          non-standard equivalents some HTC/Xiaomi ROMs send instead of
          BOOT_COMPLETED — harmless everywhere else, and this is exactly the
          class of device whose power management F1 is measuring.

          exported="false": they are only ever triggered by the system and by
          this app, so nothing else should be able to poke them.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationReceiver" />
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.dexterous.flutterlocalnotifications.ScheduledNotificationBootReceiver">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED"/>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON" />
                <action android:name="com.htc.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>

        <!--
          ===== Home-screen widget «В работе» =====

          The provider draws the last snapshot the app pushed over the
          taskradar/home_widget channel (MainActivity); the service feeds its
          list. exported="true" on the provider is required: the launcher
          sends APPWIDGET_UPDATE from another process. Row clicks therefore go
          to a second receiver that is NOT exported: only a PendingIntent this
          app created can reach it, so no other app can open a task here or
          fold a group. The service is guarded by BIND_REMOTEVIEWS, which only
          the system holds.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.TaskRadarWidgetProvider"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/widget_name">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.appwidget.action.APPWIDGET_UPDATE" />
            </intent-filter>
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.appwidget.provider"
                android:resource="@xml/taskradar_widget_info" />
        </receiver>
        <receiver
            android:name=".widget.TaskRadarWidgetClickReceiver"
            android:exported="false" />
        <service
            android:name=".widget.TaskRadarWidgetService"
            android:exported="false"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_REMOTEVIEWS" />
        <!--
          Task editor (§7): native_geofence's components, which its own manifest
          does not declare. The event receiver is reached only through the
          app's own PendingIntent and the reboot receiver only by the system,
          so neither is exported (the same reasoning as the notification
          receivers above). The reboot receiver re-registers the zones, which
          Android forgets on restart. The service is what the plugin promotes a
          geofence callback to when it needs longer; it is bound by the job
          scheduler, hence BIND_JOB_SERVICE.
        -->
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.chunkytofustudios.native_geofence.receivers.NativeGeofenceBroadcastReceiver"/>
        <receiver
            android:exported="false"
            android:name="com.chunkytofustudios.native_geofence.receivers.NativeGeofenceRebootBroadcastReceiver">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
        <service
            android:exported="false"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_JOB_SERVICE"
            android:name="com.chunkytofustudios.native_geofence.NativeGeofenceForegroundService"/>

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
        <!-- Task editor (§5): url_launcher has to see a browser to open a link
             in it; on Android 11+ an app without this entry is told there is
             none, and canLaunchUrl answers false. -->
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
            <data android:scheme="https"/>
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
            <data android:scheme="http"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
